{"adjudications":[{"id":"F1","decision":"confirmed","reason":"In the revision, 'he is a man to fear' runs straight into Julius's 'Sure that's a pity for you', so the line no longer answers a refusal. The earlier 'I daren't' comes before the whole Brown exchange. The source pairs the pity line with a renewed refusal. Restoring that two-line refusal from the source fixes the non sequitur at least cost. Kramenin's fear of Brown is already present in 'he is a man to fear'.","evidence":[{"document":"025.source","paragraph":55,"quote":"“Then you won’t do as I ask you?”"},{"document":"025.source","paragraph":56,"quote":"“You ask an impossibility.”"},{"document":"025.revision","paragraph":29,"quote":"But I know this--he is a man to fear.”"},{"document":"025.revision","paragraph":30,"quote":"“Sure that’s a pity for you,” said Julius cheerfully."}]},{"id":"F2","decision":"rejected","reason":"'No. I want Jane Finn' answers 'Do you mean to kill me?' coherently. The later 'believed implicitly in the power of money' responds to Julius's argument about dollars and the insanity defence, which survives, not to a bribe. This is a legitimate compression.","evidence":[{"document":"025.revision","paragraph":32,"quote":"You forget the dollars."},{"document":"025.revision","paragraph":33,"quote":"Corrupt himself, he believed implicitly in the power of money."}]},{"id":"F3","decision":"rejected","reason":"The concealed gun can still be inferred. The text says 'his hand in his coat pocket', and Kramenin's established cowardice explains why his nerve fails. This is an acceptable tradeoff within the word budget.","evidence":[{"document":"025.revision","paragraph":50,"quote":"Julius, his hand in his coat pocket, was urbane to the last degree"},{"document":"025.revision","paragraph":17,"quote":"The man he had to deal with was an abject physical coward"}]},{"id":"F4","decision":"rejected","reason":"Julius's opposition is still explicit: 'What the devil are you driving at?', 'Darn you!' and 'Jane, you stay where you are'. That is enough to justify the gun seizure, and it matches Jane's later line that Julius 'didn't want us to do this'.","evidence":[{"document":"025.revision","paragraph":96,"quote":"“What the devil are you driving at?” demanded Julius."},{"document":"025.revision","paragraph":100,"quote":"“Darn you!” cried Julius. “Jane, you stay where you are.”"}]}],"replacements":[{"old":"“Sure that’s a pity for you,” said Julius cheerfully.","new":"“Then you won’t do as I ask you?”

“You ask an impossibility.”

“Sure that’s a pity for you,” said Julius cheerfully.","reason":"Restore the source's renewed refusal (pars. 55–56) so that Julius's 'pity' answers it.","finding_ids":["F1"]},{"old":"and he took up the receiver, spoke for a minute or two, then turned to his employer.","new":"and he took up the receiver, then turned to his employer.","reason":"Optional cut of incidental detail in the dictation and secretary passage, which the plan marks as compressible. Offsets the restored words.","finding_ids":[]},{"old":"toes. Who’s this? Let me present","new":"toes. Let me present","reason":"Optional cut of a redundant echo of Tuppence's question. Offsets the restored words.","finding_ids":[]}],"remaining_concerns":["The net result should be about 1701 words, roughly one over the target. This is within tolerance but has not been measured."]}
